    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="2" relname="justify">I liked a definition of intuition given by the Merriam-Webster Online Dictionary as follows (Merriam-Webster Online Dictionary, n.d.):</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="33" relname="span">immediate apprehension or cognition</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="4" relname="circumstance">Also, when looking at synonyms of intuition,</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="32" relname="span">the word instinct came up as one.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="31" relname="span">This thought of intuitiveness seems to relate to how someone would be able to correctly react to a situation with little or no chance to think about it.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="35" relname="span">People could be trained to do the right thing to gain some level of intuitiveness, such as swerving to miss a bad driver.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="36" relname="span">The hope would be that there would be some reflex reaction based on previous exposure to the situation to make the driver swerve.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="9" relname="circumstance">The problem is that a bad driver can come from any direction,</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="37" relname="span">so how does the person that was trained respond to the different directions?</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="35" relname="elaboration">What about musically talented savants that just know how to play a piano without any training at all?</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="39" relname="span">How a computer application can be created to allow for intuitiveness is odd to me</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="23" relname="span">because I have time to think about what I will do and usually have the ability to reverse my actions.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="24" relname="span">However, one area of our Interaction Design textbook on page 23 that discussed mapping seemed show me what might be considered intuitive in any computer application,</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="25" relname="span">but it still related to physical movement.</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="26" relname="span">I do think that studying physical intuitive movements goes well when looking at video games and virtual simulators that can emulate physical interaction.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="28" relname="span">It was interesting that an alteration of the way a virtual figure moved in a video game led to children spending an average of 34% less time on that particular game level (Höysniemi, Hämäläinen, Turkki, &amp; Rouvi, 2005, p. 47).</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="29" relname="span">The  sideways movement of the virtual figure was changed</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="17" relname="nonvolitional-cause">because it was observed that children physically moved sideways when controlling the figure.</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="30" relname="span">This really brings home a point of how important it might be to build intuitive movements into virtual simulators</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="19" relname="nonvolitional-result">so that the person being trained will have these movements corrected and/or reinforced for real life events.</segment>
        <group id="21" type="span"/>
